{
 "id": "2026-peng-qingdao",
 "name": "Qingdao Coastal Greenway: Runners Rate Four Qualities",
 "description": "Method replication: participants imagine running at each of 10 random street views along Qingdao's coastal greenway and rate comfort, security, resilience and attractiveness from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree); the images are not the paper's stimuli (46 newly fetched Baidu street views along the coast between Qingdao Station and Fushan Bay, cropped to remove the camera car).\n\nOriginal study: Peng, Chen & Li (2026), Journal of Urban Management 15, 1365–1378. Along the coastal greenway the authors set 425 points 50 m apart and combined GoPro runner-eye-level video frames with 417 Baidu street views. On Wenjuanxing, 137 respondents gave gender, age and running frequency, then rated ten randomly shown street views on the four dimensions (5-point Likert). K-means clustering of landscape types and multiple linear regression linked blue view, green view, enclosure, walkability and other visual features to the four scores.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) only Baidu car-based street views, which follow coastal roads rather than the footpath, and no GoPro frames; (2) the running-frequency answer bands (less than once a week, 1–2 times, 3 or more) are written for this template, as the paper gives only low, moderate and high groups; (3) the one-line meaning of each dimension is shown under its label, condensed from the paper's definitions.",
